text_en:
Article 54 (Notification of phone numbers of callers)
Where a caller expresses his or her intention of rejecting transmission of his or her phone number pursuant to the proviso of Article 84(1) of the Act, no telecommunications business operator shall impose a charge on the caller for such reason.
A person who intends to learn a caller's phone number pursuant to Article 84(2)1 of the Act shall file a request with a telecommunications business operator in writing along with any of the following data by which the telecommunications business operator may confirm a specific fact that he or she has been verbally abused, threatened or ridiculed by telephone (hereinafter referred to as "threat, etc. by telephone"):
Data in which the date and hour, and details of threat, etc. by telephone have been recorded in writing;
Tape, etc. on which the details of threat, etc. by telephone have been recorded;
Where he or she has reported a crime to the police for reason of threat, etc. by telephone, documents proving such threat, etc.;
Data providing evidence that he or she has consulted with the related consultation office concerning damage caused by threat, etc. by telephone;
Other data corresponding to the provisions of subparagraphs 1 through 4.
"Cases prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 84(2)2 of the Act means cases of using the following telephone services:
For reporting international crimes for counterterrorism (111);
For reporting crimes (112);
For reporting espionage (113);
For reporting of or consultation on cyber terrorism (118);
For reporting fires or disasters (119);
For reporting maritime accidents or crimes (122);
For reporting smuggling (125);
Deleted. <May 8, 2017>
[This Article Wholly Amended on Feb. 28, 2012]

